 {"id":"BT-057","title":"[P1] The deployed boardctl was ~3 days stale and hid 96 validate findings — add a deployment freshness gate","status":"complete","priority":"P1","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T07:55:15.255449+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"FOUND 2026-09-23 (fleet review programme, subgoal 2) while proving the new validate check end-to-end. The boardctl actually on PATH (/home/kara/.local/bin/boardctl) was built from revision 79a10123 (2026-09-20T06:07:33Z, mod v0.1.7-0.20260920060733) while the repo HEAD was f3834174 (2026-09-23T07:15:10Z, mod v0.1.9-0.20260923071510) — roughly three days of committed checks were missing from every validate run the fleet performed in that window. HOW IT SURFACED: the same board was validated with both binaries. The stale one reported 'RESULT: OK (1 warning(s))' on gitreins; the current source reports OK (97 warnings): 96 of them are the BT-048 out-of-vocabulary-priority warnings that the deployed build predates. So any validate exit-code/warning count quoted from a fleet tick in that window under-reports findings — including the numbers this review programme itself recorded for other boards. Live values behind those 96: gitreins carries priority 'low' (48 rows), 'high' (26), 'medium' (22) and a bare integer (2) alongside the canonical P0-P3 — the write path normalizes bare 0-3 and case variants but not these prose values, so they persist. WHERE DID THEY COME FROM is a separate question worth a row: the board importer claims to normalize 'completed' -> 'complete' and priority to P0-P3, so an import path may be the writer. FIX APPLIED NOW, but not the durable fix: the current build was installed (vcs.revision f3834174) and gitreins re-validated at 329/329 key uniformity / exit 0. DURABLE FIX (this row): nothing catches a stale deployment — boardctl ships internal/versioncheck, so check whether a deployment-freshness gate belongs there (compare the running binary's embedded vcs.revision against the repo HEAD and warn loudly), and wire it where the fleet builds the tool. Same class as the bunker PATH-binary staleness (GAP-143) and the dagger build stamp (subgoal 5): the installed binary, not the source tree, is what produces the evidence.","detail":{"fingerprint":"f695eafeb6d906372c991af4fa4895cd0753bbed3b5175ad0aea6e1897822167"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-24T23:11:46.713196+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"complete","commit_hash":"91519d8","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"GREEN","worker_summary":"Wave worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm: internal/freshness.Check compares the ldflags-stamped buildCommit against the checkout's HEAD (walk-up .git, worktree gitfiles OK); ONE stderr warning (binary sha+date, HEAD, commits-behind count) only when the sha is an ancestor of HEAD and not HEAD; silent on empty/foreign/at-HEAD/no-git/git-missing; BOARDCTL_SKIP_FRESHNESS=1 skip; Makefile build+release stamp both wired, release refusal intact. 13 unit tests + 3 CLI wiring tests; live probes: stale warns, foreign silent, stdout byte-identical, exit 0. Merged 67d1f0c, judge PASS (fd4c6fef).","foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[],"worktree":"~/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-BT-057","branch":"wt/BT-057"}
 {"id":"BT-058","title":"P2 — the key-alias table was audited against real rows and four candidates were removed as concept-merging (plus a counter that over-reported repairs)","status":"complete","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T09:02:45.546507+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"FOUND 2026-09-23 while auditing my own instrument rather than the boards. BT-056 shipped a TaskKeyAliases table; an alias is a REPAIR INSTRUCTION that --normalize executes, so a wrong entry silently merges two concepts on every board that carries the key. Checked all 11 candidates against 57 boards / 8,443 parsed rows, comparing rows that carry BOTH the alias and its target. FOUR REMOVED: (1) commit -> commit_hash: 6 rows carry it and TWO carry commit_hash with a DIFFERENT value (commit=afffb64 vs commit_hash=9b4536e on crier CI-001) - two different commits, so the rename would have rewritten which-commit on a row that legitimately says two things; (2) deps -> depends_on: 7 rows, every value the EMPTY STRING and no depends_on anywhere - the rename puts a string where the canon expects a list of ids; (3) review_otes -> review_notes: 2 rows, 100% conflict, and the values are DIFFERENT KINDS - review_otes holds captured cell output as prose while review_notes on the same rows holds provenance notes (id-renumbered records) - THIS ALSO CORRECTS BT-056, whose text calls review_otes an outright typo of review_notes; (4) review_notes_dupe: a candidate I invented that matched no row at all. KEPT, now with evidence recorded in the code: why->reasoning, acceptance/pass_criteria/_pass_criteria/ac->acceptance_criteria (_pass_criteria: 222 rows, 0 conflicts, values are genuine pass conditions such as 'PASS: grep -r ... == 0'), pri->priority, cpx->complexity. SECOND FIX: the census and the per-row finding counted a row as fixable whenever every offending key was an alias, but RenameKey REFUSES when the canonical key is already present - including present as null - so it advertised repairs that then failed. A row is now fixable only if every offending key is a known alias AND its target is ABSENT. MEASURED EFFECT: bunker 2 fixable -> 0 (its review_otes rows are no longer sold as a mechanical fix), off-by-one keeps 10 genuine ones, gitreins unaffected at 329/329 zero drift. Regression test TestAliasWithPresentTargetIsNotAdvertisedAsFixable covers both directions. LESSON worth keeping: the alias table is the one part of this canon that WRITES, so it is held to sample evidence, not to plausibility - a plausible abbreviation ('commit', 'deps') was wrong twice out of four.","detail":{"fingerprint":"d0b88c4f41dd49d9dac666c417b0a8666f9b5d551cc934c9997201e7c8f38dda"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-23T09:02:45.556684+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":"IMPLEMENTED + VERIFIED 2026-09-23 (commit e37e1a1, pushed): four aliases removed, the fixability rule tightened, the comment block in internal/board/keys.go records the per-entry evidence, and the new regression test passes. Full repo suite green. Board: exit 0. Verified live after redeploy: gitreins 329/329 (0 drift), bunker 0 fixable (was 2), off-by-one 10 fixable, and a NULL-target row (pri beside priority:null) no longer advertises --normalize.","completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[]}
 {"id":"BT-059","title":"P2 — create's key schema is computed from the board's own rows, so a drifted board makes every NEW row inherit its drift (observed: repo:null emitted on SCHED-GAP-1575)","status":"complete","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-23T17:04:54.907902+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"OBSERVED 2026-09-23 while filing a P1 on the scheduler board. boardctl create SCHED-GAP-1575 emitted a row containing repo:null, and validate then flagged it: 'key(s) {repo} outside the declared canon'. Nothing in my create call asked for repo. The mechanism is by design and is the problem: the create schema is the create-time default set UNION the extras MEASURED FROM THAT BOARD's existing rows (the same union that feeds the key-uniformity census), so on a board that already carries drift, every new row written through the tool inherits that drift and validates with a warning. Compare GAP-145 created the same way on the bunker board minutes earlier: no repo key, because bunker's rows do not carry one. WHY IT MATTERS: the tool exists to STOP drift spreading; a write path that mirrors the drift it is measuring makes the census self-perpetuating - repairing the old rows does not stop new ones being born dirty, and a board can never reach zero drift while any legacy row carries an extra key. It also weakens the census as evidence: 'N rows drift' partly counts rows the tool itself created. SUGGESTED FIX: separate the two sets explicitly - keep a canonical create schema (what a new row SHOULD carry) apart from the measured census (what rows DO carry), and have create write only the canonical schema, leaving the census to report the difference. If a board's extra key is deliberate, it belongs in the canon (like labels, sanctioned 2026-09-23) or in the alias table - not silently inherited. ALSO SEEN while checking, filed here rather than as its own row because it is the same class: that board's SECOND validator error, 'board.jsonl line 1: EOF', is a FORMAT error - the file is pretty-printed JSON spanning many lines while the validator reads JSONL (one object per line), so line 1 can never parse. Both that error and 'duplicate task id INT-CI-006' are pre-existing (verified against the committed board: same 2 errors, 287 warnings), i.e. this row added none.","detail":{"fingerprint":"b93b8e5bb6f60dfbe9f55681bf54bbd15a112b8769f48748d8810b8d3184d65e"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-24T12:05:54.161428+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"complete","commit_hash":"8a5b04d","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"GREEN","worker_summary":"Wave worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm: Create's mirror step now intersects the last row's key set with the declared canon (IsSanctionedTaskKey) instead of copying it verbatim — new rows on a drifted board are born canon-clean (no repo:null inheritance); sanctioned extras (perpetual/labels) still flow; census unchanged (legacy rows keep warning, exit 0); 4 tests incl. clean-board byte-shape pin and explicit-fields landing. Real-board probe on a throwaway copy: 21-key row identical to the pre-fix baseline. Merged e50aea4, judge PASS.","foreman_note":"wave tick boardctl-2026-09-24-10-58-10; judge verdict .gitreins/history/2026-09-24/12c5f0e0/verdict.json","completed_at":"2026-09-24 07:04:40","sessions":["20260924_060953_b0abf6"],"depends_on":[],"worktree":"/home/kara/worktrees/coding-hermes-boardctl-BT-059","branch":"wt/BT-059"}
-{"id":"BT-060","title":"P2 — a row's priority cannot be set through the sanctioned writer: create defaults it to P2 and neither create nor update exposes --priority (or --title), so every row's level is either the default or hand-edited","status":"pending","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-24T15:14:38.894296+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":null,"detail":{"fingerprint":"5374c9a7be5bc5d2be23868011ba0d5394a2315fdabc7889b5286d170bb9b6f3"},"complexity":3,"updated_at":"2026-09-25T13:09:22.681325+00:00","capability_tags":[],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":"Correction 2026-09-25 (found while fixing BT-064): 'boardctl create' DOES accept --priority/--status/--reasoning even though `boardctl help create` does not list them - proven by writing --priority P1 into a scratch board (default is P2, the row came back P1) and --reasoning text persisting. So the claim 'create cannot express priority' is WRONG and the D6 title/priority contradictions are not explained by a hard refusal; they are explained by a caller writing a priority-bearing TITLE while leaving the field at its default P2, which nothing validates. Ask unchanged and now narrower: (1) print every accepted flag in help, (2) add --title to update, (3) warn in validate when a title carries a Pn token that disagrees with the priority field.","completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[],"worktree":null,"branch":null}
-{"id":"BT-061","title":"[P2] SCOPE: coding-hermes-boardctl — decide what connecting over the muster protocol means for this service","status":"pending","priority":"P2","type":null,"created_at":"2026-09-24T15:16:47.782659+00:00","created_by":null,"reasoning":"CONTEXT — what \"connect over muster\" means here (read before scoping).\n\nmuster (github.com/wojons/muster) turns any OpenAPI 3.x spec into a typed CLI\n(openapi-cli), an MCP server (openapi-mcp), a daemon, and a Go library. A consumer\nattaches with one config block:\n\n  {\"mcpServers\": {\"<name>\": {\"command\": \"openapi-mcp\",\n                             \"args\": [\"--spec\", \"<url-or-path>\", \"--base-url\", \"<api>\"]}}}\n\n(muster README \"mcpServers\" + docs/integration-guide.md §2; verified 2026-09-24.)\n\nDESIGN AUTHORITY for every protocol row: muster/specs/PROTOCOLS.md — \"no protocol\nimplementation row may start before its adapter satisfies this spec's pipeline and\ncross-cutting contracts on paper\". The pipeline is discover/ingest -> protocol-neutral\noperation model -> SHARED command+MCP generation -> per-protocol transport executor.\nOpenAPI 3.x is the FIRST adapter, not the engine. muster's board carries PROTO-001..008.\n\n\"Support for people to connect to us over the muster protocol\" therefore means, for this\nproject: (1) publish a muster-consumable OpenAPI 3.x contract for our own service,\n(2) ship the consumer config + a human quickstart, (3) PROVE muster's own binaries\ningest it and drive the live service. Nothing here re-implements muster — we consume it.\n\n             THIS ROW IS ONE THIRD OF A CHAIN (scope -> spec -> build) filed together\n2026-09-24 at the operator's request. Do not start this row until its predecessor\nhas landed; the spec row must not invent a surface the scope row has not decided, and\nthe build row must not guess a contract the spec row has not frozen.\n\n             MEASURED STATE OF CODING-HERMES-BOARDCTL TODAY (2026-09-24, read from the live tree):\n\n             boardctl has an HTTP serve mode but publishes NO contract — measured 2026-09-24: `boardctl serve\n[-C dir] [--addr 127.0.0.1:8787]` exists in the CLI verb list, and grepping cmd/ and internal/ for\nopenapi/OpenAPI returns nothing. So the surface a muster consumer would attach to exists, but its\ncontract is undocumented and unpublished. This repo has unusually strong existing machinery to\nbuild on, and the scope should reuse it rather than invent: internal/describe/registry.go is the\nONE source of --help, `describe --json` and docs/reference.md, cmd/gendocs/ generates the reference\nfrom that registry (\"never hand-written\"), and there is already a route/endpoint parity test\ndiscipline in this repo. A spec generated from the serve route table is the shape that matches.\nBoard rows are the foreman's exclusive responsibility in this fleet (workers must not mutate them),\nso the MCP-safety boundary is not hypothetical here — it is the repo's own standing rule.\n\n             Specific scope questions: (a) which serve routes are GET-only projections (list/show/render/validate)\nversus mutations, and are mutations exposed to a muster consumer at all? This repo's standing rule\nis that board writes are the foreman's alone, so the default must be read-only and the row must say\nso explicitly rather than leaving it to the generator. (b) spec location: generated and committed as\na file, or served live from the route table? Name the parity check either way (this repo already\nfails its build on docs/routes drift — reuse that shape). (c) does `serve` need to answer the\nspec route so a consumer can point --spec straight at the running daemon, and what does that imply\nfor loopback-only binding?\nNOTE: this project also owns the fleet-wide board tooling — if a shared muster config or spec\ngenerator emerges from any of these four chains, boardctl is the natural home for it, and the scope\nrow should say whether it should generalise.\n\n             DELIVERABLE — a written scope, on this row (no code):\n             1. the surface we expose and why that surface (and, explicitly, what we do NOT expose)\n             2. the exact muster consumer block (the mcpServers entry with its real --spec path/URL\n                and --base-url) written out verbatim\n             3. the acceptance test for the later rows: the precise commands a verifier runs, and\n                the assertions that make them non-vacuous\n             4. the boundary call: which operations are agent-safe through MCP and which must be\n                gated (muster's MCP safety contract — mutating operations an agent should not\n                reach unattended)\n             5. the auth story: this service's credential/header vs what muster's config can carry\n\n             ACCEPTANCE: the row closes with the five items above present and concrete enough\n             that the spec row needs no new decision. If a real decision is still open, say so\n             and name who rules on it.","detail":{"fingerprint":"4477c3b94450238dc3da9e16e388bb912e3251cc0d960fa06a613b0e30316a3a"},"complexity":2,"updated_at":"2026-09-24T15:16:47.782659+00:00","capability_tags":["+integration","+protocol","+interop"],"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"foreman_note":null,"completed_at":null,"sessions":null,"depends_on":[],"worktree":null,"branch":null}
